is the image of the line shown above under the action of this transformation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535925 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "072c2798-6d16-5d14-9659-f337b6ce7515" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535925 #edb/ref 1074 "A dilation leaves a line passing through the center of the dilation unchanged.\n\nTherefore, the image ${l}^{′}$ of the line $l$ under the dilation  $(x,y)↦(1.5x,1.5y)$ is the line $l$ itself, as shown below. \n\n![](images/b4/b48fecb9d9286ac4df6a9bfbf151fd78a98d223f7375e5251cda1119f8f66611.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535927 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535927 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125779" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535927 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535927 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/14/14ca2d4167ff6950d5a9d7bb41cc856ecbc211a033518b8b366ac977953700b1.png)\n\nThe point ${P}^{′}(3,1.5)$ is the image of the point $P(-4,-2)$ under a dilation with the center at the origin, as shown above. What is the scale factor of the transformation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535927 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0b671036-c3a4-5237-8125-3af3f125754a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535927 #edb/ref 1074 "A dilation with center at the origin and a factor of $k$ can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(kx,ky)\\,.\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the point $P$ and equating the result to ${P}^{′},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k\\cdot (-4),k\\cdot (-2)=(3,1.5)\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-4k=3 \\\\ -2k=1.5.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\frac{3}{-4}=\\frac{1.5}{-2}=-\\frac{3}{4}.$   \n\n![](images/d3/d3b818151bcfddd3fd4f642463ba4e06ba1844d4b772652dadde0d3049e441ee.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535935 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535935 #edb/ref 1069 "q-89713" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535935 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535935 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/4c/4c3e53db0b578b306a12711c4e69a0d75b850cb1eecc8d5b0e6df79acc494d7a.png)\n\nA dilation with center $O$ is defined by the function $(x,y)↦(0.2x,0.2y).$  What is the image of the line shown above under the action of this transformation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535935 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "19060a1c-49c3-55db-84f0-fb34722058a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535935 #edb/ref 1074 "A dilation leaves a line passing through the center of the dilation unchanged.\n\nTherefore, the image ${l}^{′}$ of the line $l$ under the dilation  $(x,y)↦(0.2x,0.2y)$ is the line $l$ itself, as shown below. \n\n![](images/1a/1ab7188d164a491720d4d9d361abd4c2c76ea2cb09f6edbea3b0790a6281a785.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535948 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535948 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125795" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535948 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535948 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/bf/bfcd52f31f825e6445700e8495f7acbfce46139144f8bb61e977b2971e01d877.png)\n\nThe larger polygon shown above is the image of the smaller one under the action of a dilation. Find the center of the dilation and its scale factor $k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535948 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "527b9edb-ee7b-5004-b192-96e29f02e1cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535948 #edb/ref 1074 "The center of a dilation lies on any line which passes through a point and its image.\n\nTherefore, we can find the center of our dilation as, for example, the point of intersection of the lines $\\overset{⟷}{B{B}^{′}}$ and $\\overset{⟷}{C{C}^{′}},$ as shown below.  \n\n![](images/d7/d7215165822a95ce9689e3666ff6ea9454048a936c372b07a6224d199b8d0ed7.png)\n\nFrom the diagram above, where we have that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{B}^{′}{C}^{′}}{BC}=\\frac{6}{2}=3,\n$$\n\nand the same proportion holds for the other corresponding sides too. As a result, the absolute value of the scale factor is \n\n$$\n|k|=3.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, notice that our figures have different orientations, which means that the scale factor of the dilation is negative.\n\nTherefore, the scale factor is $k=-3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535953 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535953 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125558" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535953 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535953 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b8/b8730b1936d1ff9f0c8d3845a70327e351b23c7085f248bcb6e99e73f0cb8c9e.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the point $(8,-4)$ under the action of a dilation with center $O$ and scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535953 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5e9dde9d-e070-5830-aa05-46ebbed13863" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535953 #edb/ref 1074 "A dilation with center at the origin and a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ can be represented by the function  \n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(\\frac{1}{2}x,\\frac{1}{2}y).\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the point $(8,-4),$ we have\n\n$$\n(8,-4)↦(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-4))=(4,-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resulting point is $(4,-2).$\n\n![](images/c2/c2c00f1380ce776da3fe4e1ab2b30c969d5d7436966d907db7a0ceb5f0a13f33.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535980 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535980 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125692" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535980 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535980 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/98/98e64a245737736bfc987fe9230b1ee3fc81eef4aedfea8c2e5e9bcf949f8203.png)\n\nWhat is the image of the polygon shown above under a dilation that is centered at the origin and has a scale factor of $-\\frac{2}{3}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535980 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a76fa1c3-fa48-5812-bf8b-fa26865dd75b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535980 #edb/ref 1074 "The dilation with center at the origin and a scale factor of $-\\frac{2}{3}$ can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(-\\frac{2}{3}x,-\\frac{2}{3}y).\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the vertices $(-3,-3),$ $(0,0),$  and $(-3,3),$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3,-3) & ↦(-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-3),\\,-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-3))=(2,2) \\\\ (0,0) & ↦(-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 0,\\,-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 0)=(0,0) \\\\ (-3,3) & ↦(-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-3),\\,-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 3)=(2,-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following polygon.\n\n![](images/29/2921272ffd1c38c082bf2123086d8c7b19a910c91d132810042b86728b820f8d.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535992 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535992 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125574" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535992 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535992 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/84/841dfcfabb27fe93002cb05c8d84ad9da2e0a9f6b810476e7964651e515e757c.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the point $(8,-6)$ under the action of a dilation with center $O$ and scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535992 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cac69546-49cc-507e-802a-92a175e045ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535992 #edb/ref 1074 "A dilation with center at the origin and a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(\\frac{1}{2}x,\\frac{1}{2}y).\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the point $(8,-6),$ we have\n\n$$\n(8,-6)↦(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-6))=(4,-3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resulting point is $(4,-3).$\n\n![](images/53/53df2214415b81380c96af98b2d073e2a16480715386926603cd471eec66dec6.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535997 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535997 #edb/ref 1069 "q-88115" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535997 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535997 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/3a/3a512ef7399ac46c611335a9f957bfae9a14e802ddd39e492f6184e0e149fb7e.png)\n\nWhat is the image of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ under a dilation that is centered at the origin and has a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535997 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d78079e7-2f05-54ed-bf64-8723ddffd37e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535997 #edb/ref 1074 "A dilation with center at the origin and a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(\\frac{1}{3}x,\\frac{1}{3}y).\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the endpoints $A(-6,-3)$ and $B(3,6),$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6,-3) & ↦(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-6),\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot -3)=(-2,-1) \\\\ (3,6) & ↦(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (3),\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 6)=(1,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resulting points are ${A}^{′}(-2,-1)$ and ${B}^{′}(1,2).$\n\nFinally, we connect ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}$ using a segment, as shown below. \n\n![](images/ba/ba27f325ca104397544c7b5d19078d59cf63209a83d89de73cdeb752f5c0fc84.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
